Add security to the backend using Spring Security. Do not weaken or remove existing tests; update them where needed.

Design (state your choice and a one-paragraph rationale before coding): stateless JWT authentication, with tokens delivered in an httpOnly, Secure (in non-dev), SameSite cookie if feasible; otherwise Authorization header with short expiry. Do not store tokens in localStorage on the frontend.

Requirements:
1. User document and repository in a separate "users" collection with unique username index; passwords hashed with BCrypt (or Argon2).
2. Endpoints: POST /api/v1/auth/login, POST /api/v1/auth/logout, GET /api/v1/auth/me.
3. Roles USER (read-only access to products) and ADMIN (full CRUD). Enforce with method or request-level authorization. /stats requires authentication.
4. Seed two dev-only users (admin and user) with passwords from environment variables, never hard-coded.
5. CORS restricted to a configurable list of origins (the React dev origin in dev).
6. Security headers (content type options, frame options, etc.), and CSRF handling that matches the chosen token strategy.
7. Login rate limiting or throttling (e.g., Bucket4j or a simple in-memory filter).
8. Prevent NoSQL injection: no query built from raw user-provided strings or operators; validate/whitelist sort fields and filter parameters.
9. JWT secret and expiry from environment variables, with startup failure if the secret is missing or too short outside the test profile.
10. Restrict Actuator: only /health exposed publicly, with details hidden.
11. Make sure errors for 401/403 are ProblemDetail responses and logs never contain passwords or tokens.

Add tests: login success/failure, 401 for anonymous, 403 for USER on write endpoints, 200 for ADMIN, and an attempted operator-injection payload being rejected. Run the full build and summarize the security decisions in a SECURITY.md.